      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2876.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 738,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, we have to look at it through a few different lenses: its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird and Wonderful)\nWater is one of the most unique substances in the universe. Unlike almost any other chemical, it behaves in ways that defy normal rules:\n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why water carries nutrients, minerals, and chemicals wherever it goes—in our bloodstreams, through plant roots, and in our oceans.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different. When it freezes into ice, it actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life, and life on Earth as we know it wouldn't exist.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet. \n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite all that water, only about **2.5% is fresh water** (not salty). And of that tiny fraction, over 68% is locked away in glaciers and ice caps, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use in rivers, lakes, and underground aquifers.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the **hydrologic cycle**. It evaporates from the oceans into the vapor, forms clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows back to the sea through rivers. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nSimply put, **life cannot exist without water.**\n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are roughly 31% water. \n* **Metabolism:** Water is the medium in which all the cellular and chemical reactions of life take place. Without it, digestion, circulation, and temperature regulation would stop immediately.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals, forming complex aquatic ecosystems that produce much of the oxygen we breathe.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a humble, transparent, odorless liquid, yet it is the ultimate driving force of geology, climate, and biology. It is the cradle of life on Earth and the single most important resource for human survival."
